AB  - Natural language to SQL (NL2SQL) enables non-expert users to query relational databases using natural language, but existing methods often remain insufficient for practical deployment owing to limited accuracy and unstable behavior under ambiguous or repeated queries. This paper presents an NL2SQL framework that integrates a large language model (LLM) with retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) and a semantic cache. The LLM serves as the primary SQL generator, while an explicitly controlled combination of RAG and semantic caching augments the LLM with external evidence and selective, safety-aware reuse of prior generation experiences. By leveraging retrieved knowledge and high-similarity historical query-SQL pairs, the framework improves the robustness and consistency of SQL generation under diverse and paraphrase-heavy user queries. Experiments on the public Spider benchmark show that the proposed framework consistently improves execution-based SQL generation quality over weaker pipeline variants, while the semantic-cache branch further supports efficient reuse under repeated semantic intents.
