AB  - The current Retrieval-Augmented Generation(RAG) pipeline incorporates retrieved contexts as external knowledge to reduce hallucinations, but the large and redundant context makes it difficult for the model to identify key information. In Question-Answering (QA) tasks, large language models (LLMs) can identify query-focused information through extractive compression, simplify redundant long texts and improve the accuracy, but they are resource-intensive and less efficient. To ensure the accuracy of extractive compression and improve its efficiency, we propose a framework for query-focused extractive compression (QEC-LLM) that leverages the LLM’s knowledge and a queryguided mechanism to construct sample sets, effectively training a smaller student model. The student model sorts and scales paragraph sentences based on similarity scores for compression and performance optimization. In addition, since LLM may generate some information that is missing from the student model during the extraction process, our framework will add this supplementary information to the QA prompt and combine it with the extracted information to achieve generative augmentation. We conduct extensive experiments on QA datasets with different compression ratios. The results demonstrate that QECLLM significantly improves end-to-end QA performance and inference efficiency, while demonstrating strong generalization across different retrieval strategies.
