AB  - The rapid integration of Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and multi-agent Large Language Models (LLM) is reshaping the healthcare sector, enhancing clinical decision support and patient triage. While these systems enhance reasoning through dynamic knowledge retrieval and agent collaboration, they introduce significant privacy vulnerabilities regarding Protected Health Information (PHI). This paper presents a systematic review and architectural risk analysis of centralized RAG, on-device RAG, and distributed multi-agent structures published between 2023 and 2025. Unlike prior surveys that focus primarily on single-agent chatbots, this work explores how agent cooperation, memory retention, and multi-hop retrieval create emergent PHI leakage vectors. We introduce a formal mathematical risk propagation model to quantify these vulnerabilities and instantiate it through a synthetic Monte Carlo simulation that characterizes how exposure scales across architectures, governance weightings, and multi-hop propagation paths. We further contextualize the model against recently published empirical benchmarks of multi-agent privacy leakage, showing that its qualitative prediction-that inter-agent channels are a substantial source of exposure-is consistent with directly measured leakage rates in independent studies. We conclude by proposing specific benchmarking metrics and privacy-by-architecture principles to support HIPAA-compliant agentic AI deployments.
