AB  - Current AI benchmarks often focus on abstract reasoning and logic rather than the applied skills that professional human workers need on their job. They also frequently suffer from data contamination, as public test sets are often included in model training data. To address this, we present a proof-of-concept pipeline that automatically converts PDF exams into structured AI benchmarks and apply this framework to nonpublic Swiss Federal Professional Exams, representing a first step toward systematic national-level assessment of AI capabilities across professional domains. Our approach uses multimodal Large Language Models to convert the exams into standardized machine-readable formats and an LLM-as-a-judge system to grade a model candidate's answers against official solutions. We evaluated a range of frontier and open-weight models, finding a clear performance hierarchy where frontier models significantly outperform smaller open models. Additionally, we explored the impact of RetrievalAugmented Generation (RAG), observing that while it substantially benefits smaller models, it offers limited gains for frontier models which likely already possess more domain knowledge. This work describes a reproducible method for creating robust AI benchmarks from suitable exams in PDF format, and we provide our pipeline as an open-source tool for the community.11Repository: https://github.com/bkressig/exam-to-ai-benchmark
