AB  - Deploying large language model (LLM)-based information retrieval systems in any organizational setting introduces critical challenges in security, access control, and providing factual information. We present SECUIR (Secure Information Retrieval), a secure, access-controlled information retrieval framework designed for multi-tenant organizational use and deployment. SECUIR addresses three practical security and reliability concerns: (1) multi-tenant data isolation via per-organization vector datastores with URI-prefix enforcement, preventing cross-organizational data leakage; (2) inputscope enforcement via a dual-gate embedding-based topic filter that rejects out-of-scope and adversarially crafted queries quickly with zero API cost, preventing vocabulary-overlap bypass attacks; and (3) incorrect-answer suppression via factual grounding, reducing false or unsupported claims in outputs. Our system is proposed as a hardened model by removing six vulnerabilities, including Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF), LLM prompt injection via history manipulation, cross-org corpus leakage, and topic index sampling bias. A 100-question institutional benchmark (judge: Claude Sonnet 4, with human review; Cohen’s $\kappa=0.763$) shows SECUIR achieves 91.9% F1 versus $\mathbf{6 1 . 5 \%}$ for direct LLM generation (+30.4 pp), with a 40.5 pp recall advantage. The system was experimented to host reliably across five organizations (504-20,003 documents) with $\mathcal{O}(\Delta)$ incremental indexing enabling deployment in under 10 minutes.
ER  - 

