AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation has become a widely adopted paradigm for grounding language model outputs in external knowledge. A persistent limitation is that the standard pipeline passes all retrieved documents to the generator without assessing their relevance, and prior work has shown that noisy context can degrade performance below the no-retrieval baseline. This paper presents CorrRAG, a corrective framework built around two contributions: a lightweight attention-based evaluator that classifies retrieval quality by operating on cross-encoder hidden representations rather than relying on fixed score thresholds, and a systematic comparison of four query rewriting strategies—LLM rewriting, pseudo-relevance feedback, hypothetical document embeddings, and keyword expansion— conducted for the first time under controlled conditions within a corrective RAG setting. Experiments on Natural Questions and TriviaQA demonstrate consistent improvements over strong baselines. On TriviaQA, CorrRAG-Attention achieves the best performance across all three metrics, outperforming Naive RAG by 3.6 EM points and 0.9 F1 points. On NQ, it achieves the highest EM and Accuracy among all compared methods, with CorrRAG-Threshold recording a marginally higher F1, reflecting a trade-off between correction precision and answer coverage that is examined in the experimental analysis.
ER  - 

