AB  - Tea-tourism convergence, in which traditional tea production is combined with cultural-heritage and rural-tourism services, is an emerging form of smart-economy practice in rural revitalization. In practice, however, data from tea plantations, scenic operation and visitor reviews remain highly siloed. Existing AI chatbots and tourism platforms also suffer from hallucination, unstructured large-language-model (LLM) outputs and a lack of explainable forecasting. This paper presents a unified big-data and LLM-driven platform that integrates three modules. The first is a domain-constrained RAG module that reduces the LLM hallucination rate from 21.0% to 3.3% on a 30-query domain benchmark. The second is a schema-constrained LLM route planner backed by a deterministic rule-based fallback, which achieves 100% service availability under LLM-side failures. The third is a real MQTT-based IoT telemetry pipeline in which four ESP8266 wireless nodes deployed in a tea plantation stream live environmental readings - continuous temperature and humidity together with threshold (on/off) soil-moisture, light and air-quality states - through an EMQX broker to a FastAPI consumer, which fans them out to browser clients over Server-Sent Events under a unified publish-subscribe contract. The three modules are coupled at the data level: the conversational assistant is grounded in the live MQTT telemetry as well as the knowledge base, so visitors can query current field conditions and receive sensor-backed answers. The system is built on FastAPI + LangChain + FAISS + DeepSeek-R1 with an EMQX MQTT broker, and it ingests live field telemetry rather than synthetic frames. We present it as an application-level systems prototype and therefore do not claim a hardware performance benchmark.
ER  - 

