AB  - Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly applied in healthcare for clinical question answering and information support, yet their deployment is constrained by risks of hallucinated content and embedded bias that can compromise patient safety. This study proposes an integrated mitigation framework that combines advanced prompt engineering with knowledge-grounded approaches, specifically Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) and the Model Context Protocol (MCP), without modifying underlying model parameters. Using a mixed-methods design, parallel RAG and MCP pipelines were implemented with shared retrieval and prompting logic and evaluated on NIH-derived MedQuAD subsets (CancerQA and NIDDKQA). Structured prompting strategies including role-based prompts, chain-of-thought, and chain-of-verification were applied consistently across both pipelines. Quantitative evaluation using accuracy, semantic similarity, evidence-backed rate, abstention, recall, and demographic drift metrics shows that MCP consistently outperforms or matches conventional RAG, achieving higher F1 and ROUGE-L scores, stronger evidence grounding, and lower abstention while maintaining minimal demographic drift. Expert review further confirmed improved clinical relevance and reduced unsupported claims. These findings demonstrate that coupling structured prompting with knowledge grounding produces more reliable, safer, and fairer healthcare LLM outputs, offering a practical and scalable pathway for trustworthy AI deployment in high-stakes clinical settings.
