AB  - While there is some evidence that large language models (LLMs) can facilitate psychoeducation, symptom monitoring, structured self-help, and between-session care, evidence from the previous generation of rule-based or retrieval-based chatbots does not necessarily carry over to generative models. This narrative review involved a search of six databases for the literature from 2021-2026, and the identification of evidence by architecture, clinical role, autonomy, population, and study design. It also covers issues of retrieval grounding, safety filtering, crisis detection, personalization, human oversight, and life cycle monitoring. Existing evidence is limited, inconsistent, and focused on short-term studies for LLMs. The proposed Mental-Health LLM Lifecycle Assurance Framework is based on the principles of WHO and the APA, which are operationalized through five operational gates (define, verify, pilot, evaluate, and monitor), with measurable criteria for factuality, crisis safety, privacy, fairness, dependence, drift, and escalation. LLM chatbots therefore should be assessed as a change in clinical-technical systems of care and an additional supervised tool, not as a substitute for professional care.
