AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable progress in natural language processing and show strong potential for medical question answering (QA). Their ability to process biomedical literature and generate coherent responses makes them attractive for clinical and research applications. However, their deployment in high-stakes domains is limited due to critical issues such as hallucinations, outdated knowledge, and lack of verifiability. These shortcomings undermine reliability and highlight the need for mechanisms that can ensure factual grounding. We propose a lightweight post-hoc verification framework that integrates LLMs with biomedical Knowledge Graphs (KGs). Using the Gene Ontology (GO) as a domain-specific resource, our framework extracts factual statements from LLM-generated responses, reformulates them as KG queries, and validates them against curated biomedical knowledge. Unsupported claims are flagged or corrected through an optional refinement loop, improving factual consistency without requiring retraining or fine-tuning of the base LLM. We evaluate the framework on two datasets with different characteristics: PubMedQA, a benchmark of general biomedical questions derived from PubMed abstracts, and GO-QA, a domain-specific dataset constructed from Gene Ontology concepts. Results show consistent improvements in factual accuracy, with BERT-F1 increasing from 0.4110 to 0.4245 on PubMedQA and from 0.8242 to 0.8405 on GO-QA. These findings demonstrate that posthoc KG verification is a practical and generalizable strategy for enhancing the reliability and trustworthiness of LLM-based medical QA systems.
