AB  - Large language models (LLMs) are becoming central to production pipelines at companies of all sizes. At scale, however, a tradeoff emerges: more capable models complete individual tasks more successfully, but charge per-token rates linearly proportional to that capacity. Billions of tokens are served daily at production companies, making this tradeoff one of cost versus quality. Companies that serve millions of requests per day cannot afford cost overruns or spikes in latency. Routing all traffic through a single large model is costly because many requests are simple. In practice, companies use heuristic approaches or manual rulesets that do not transfer well between workflows. In this paper, we address this challenge by presenting CALLS (Cost-Aware LLM Load-balancing Strategy), a strategy for selecting one of a fleet of model tiers for each query based on its estimated complexity. CALLS offers: (i) a lightweight complexity classifier that uses features capturing the syntax and semantics of the input query, (ii) a simple yet expressive three-tier routing policy based on a formalization of the cost-utility tradeoff, and (iii) an online feedback loop that calibrates the complexity thresholds used in the classifier based on observed response quality. We evaluate CALLS on four classes of enterprise tasks: retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), code assistance, long-document summarization, and question answering, and demonstrate that it decreases inference cost on a corpus of 48k production queries by 61.4% while losing only 3.2% of its quality relative to serving all traffic through a large model. Compared with state-of-the-art adaptive nearest cohort techniques, CALLS yields an additional 14.7% improvement with negligible quality loss.
ER  - 

