AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate impressive capabilities in general-purpose text generation although they face serious reliability challenges in financial analysis-a domain where precision and currency are non-negotiable. The knowledge cutoff inherent in training data, coupled with a tendency towards hallucination, presents unacceptable risks for systems intended to support investment decisions. This study introduces a novel hybrid Question-Answering architecture designed specifically for Borsa Istanbul (BIST) data, fusing LLM-based generation with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and an Intent Classification layer. To support this, we constructed a proprietary dataset comprising approximately 9,000 documents sourced from recent financial news and BIST100 reports. A key innovation involves the handling of structured tabular data, such as Public Disclosure Platform (KAP) filings; these were integrated into the vector space using a specialized preprocessing pipeline to maintain semantic integrity. The resulting system utilizes a GPT-4o-mini powered intent layer to refine queries and a FAISS-based vector search engine to construct context within milliseconds. To evaluate the efficacy of the proposed architecture, three open-source models with distinct parameter structures (Zephyr-7B, Turkish-Llama-8B, OpenR1-Qwen-7B) and GPT-4o-mini as a reference model were subjected to comparative analysis in both context-free (LLM Only) and retrieval-augmented (LLM + RAG) scenarios. Experimental results demonstrate that RAG integration reduced hallucination rates from approximately 70% to the 10% range and improved accuracy metrics by nearly threefold. Furthermore, the Turkish-Llama 8B model outperformed the commercial GPT-4o-mini model in the retrieval-augmented scenario, achieving the highest performance, thereby experimentally validating the effectiveness of localized models in financial analysis.
ER  - 

