AB  - Indonesian communities have used herbal medicine, including jamu, for centuries; therefore, the challenge is not to introduce herbal care but to help people use it more safely and systematically. This study presents an Indonesian-language web chatbot that performs humanized anamnesis before producing herbal recommendations and benchmarks two locally deployed large language models—deepseek-r1:7b and gemma4:latest—inside a safety-constrained retrieval-augmented pipeline. The design contributes an anamnesis-first controller with a three-question budget and anti-repetition memory, a deterministic and auditable multi-model ranking that avoids LLM-as-judge scoring, and an operational benchmark drawn from real runtime logs rather than static prompt replay. On a log snapshot of 113 dual-model interactions, Gemma was selected more often (42 vs. 39 wins) and had a higher successful response rate (90.0% vs. 75.0%), while DeepSeek remains stronger for follow-up question generation—indicating that model strength depends on the dialogue stage. The system targets education and early symptom exploration, not diagnosis, and redirects critical and internal medicine cases to professional care.
