AB  - While Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated advanced reasoning capabilities, their deployment in safety-critical healthcare settings requires rigorous evaluation beyond predictive performance. In Clinical Decision Support (CDS), LLMs can act as reasoners that can process longitudinal patient data and explore the impact of hypothetical treatment scenarios. However, predictive accuracy alone is insufficient when such systems are used to simulate ‘what-if’ outcomes under alter-native interventions where ground truth observations don’t exist. Ensuring counterfactual robustness under input perturbations is essential for safe deployment.Counterfactual evaluation of LLMs, especially for time-series forecasting, is a growing but underexplored area. Existing work largely focuses on fairness auditing under demographic perturbations or on factual consistency in static question-answering settings. There is no established framework for evaluating LLM responses and reasoning to intervention-like perturbations in longitudinal clinical care where ground truth outcomes are unobservable and model consistency is the only available signal [1]. This gap is particularly consequential in HIV care where treatment decisions are sequential, confounded by patient history, and carry significant downstream consequences.In prior work, we conducted a case study in HIV care evaluating LLM behavior on a longitudinal forecasting task using structured patient histories. Using the HealthGym synthetic dataset [2], we evaluated four state-of-the-art LLMs on next step prediction of Viral Load (VL), CD4 and Relative CD4 under immutable perturbations to patient gender, ethnicity [3] and a mutable perturbation to base drug combination. Agreement-based analyses were used to characterize model behavior under controlled modifications to demographic attributes and treatment regimens. Findings indicated that counterfactual stability is strongly outcome-dependent and that numerical divergence can coexist with apparent clinical agreement, revealing failure modes that accuracy-based evaluations do not capture [4].Building on this, this research project aims to develop principled methodologies for both evaluating and improving counter-factual reasoning in LLM-based clinical systems. Specifically, our study aims are to address (1) How can stability and divergence across models be systematically quantified under intervention-like perturbations in longitudinal data? (2) Can targeted fine-tuning strategies that utilize synthetic trajectory data reduce the sensitivity of the model to clinical perturbations while maintaining predictive reliability? (3) Can the evaluation framework developed in HIV care be applied to other longitudinal clinical settings, and what adaptations are required?To address these questions, in the short term, we plan to construct controlled evaluation pipelines using publicly available longitudinal datasets, with perturbations grounded in clinically meaningful intervention scenarios. Fairness-aware analysis will be incorporated to audit sensitivity to demographic attributes. Synthetic data generation approaches will be explored both for stress-testing models and enriching training data. In the long term, we will investigate the portability of this evaluation frame-work to other longitudinal clinical settings where intervention effects are clinically meaningful, adapting perturbation design and stability metrics to domain-specific requirements.Expected contributions include: (1) a principled evaluation framework for counterfactual robustness in LLM-aided clinical decision support, (2) fine-tuning strategies to reduce model sensitivity while maintaining predictive reliability, and (3) empirical evidence on the portability of the evaluation framework across longitudinal clinical domains.This work has implications across three levels. For HIV care, it provides clinicians and system designers with concrete tools for auditing LLM reliability before deployment in treatment planning and treatment adherence support workflows. More broadly, the proposed evaluation framework offers a reusable methodology that can be extended to other longitudinal CDS tasks, establishing a standard for robustness assessment that complements existing accuracy-based benchmarks. From an AI methods perspective, this work contributes to the emerging literature on consistency-based reliability evaluation [5], positioning inter-model agreement under perturbation as a proxy for sensitivity evaluation in settings where ground truth is unavailable.
ER  - 

