AB  - Financial decision-makers such as CFOs, business owners, and analysts often pose high-level, ambiguous questions that require multi-step data analysis and synthesis. Existing structured-data question answering systems can handle straightforward financial queries but struggle with complex analytical tasks due to limited query decomposition, multi-hop reasoning, and narrative report generation. We introduce BizQuery, a generalizable, iterative agentic framework that orchestrates Large Language Models (LLMs) with a structured data interface to answer complex financial inquiries. BizQuery decomposes high-level questions into actionable sub-questions, builds a knowledge base via parallelized querying, and iteratively tests its sufficiency using a hybrid of LLM-based reflection and embedding-based semantic checks. A secure data analysis sub-agent performs multi-table reasoning through a declarative JSON specification, improving transparency and avoiding code execution risks. BizQuery then synthesizes its findings into coherent, data-grounded narrative reports that users can iteratively refine through natural language feedback. We propose a complementary evaluation framework that measures both sub-question quality and final report accuracy using LLM judges, semantic similarity metrics, and hallucination checks. Across multiple real-world financial datasets, BizQuery achieves consistent improvements over strong baselines in factual accuracy, analytical depth, and report quality, suggesting its potential as a scalable solution for advanced financial analysis.
