AB  - The adoption of Large Language Model (LLMs) has led to discoveries in conversational agents, yet a critical, though, that in so far as it's going about to be so critical as to be limit exists: the contextual amnesia does. Most chatbots work in solo sessions that lack persistent memory, that leads to repetitive interaction - lack of personalised understanding. A hybrid memory framework has been presented in this paper that is designed to overcome this challenge by combining automated semantic retrieval using the explicitly provided user-curated knowledge. Our approach integrates RAG - Retrieval-Augmented Generation engine that accessing pertinent past conversations to a users managed knowledge vault that is, information snippets homes that keep key information. Implemented using cloud native infrastructure, vector db and a core LLM, this architecture allows conversational agents to maintain”- long term, stateful memory. The system proposed turns the stateless AI assistants to proactive partners that learn and grow with their N users Providing a scalable and user-centric solution to enhance Ray H. Boyd, PhD, who was not involved in the study, and whose group develops conversational AI experiences, said that “AI, whether conversational or not, represents one of the emerging front areas of HCI.”
